d-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The purpose clause has its own subject **you** and modal **could**.","**So that** introduces a finite purpose clause whose subject is you.","Choose **so that**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"In order to needs a base verb immediately, not you could.","B":"Because of cannot introduce this finite purpose clause.","C":"Despite both changes the relation and requires a different complement.","D":"**So that** introduces a finite purpose clause whose subject is you."}}},{"id":"6d143bd8-9405-5bae-adb4-cceab6c7ae52","topicSlug":"linking-clauses","level":"B1","prompt":"Use the time preposition before an -ing noun phrase: “The error occurred during the process of ____ the evening meal.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"preparing"},{"id":"B","text":"prepare"},{"id":"C","text":"to prepare"},{"id":"D","text":"prepared"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"The preposition **of** needs a noun-like complement: use the **-ing form**.","explanation":"After preposition **of**, use **preparing** to name the process.","keywords":["during-while"],"skill":"during-while","contextKey":"linking-clauses-49","fingerprint":"1060d41ba8ab45e1352d786c3c9d5f8de3420f3ee6750832544c18cfad8d0830","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["dfe-grammar"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The preposition **of** needs a noun-like complement: use the **-ing form**.","After preposition **of**, use **preparing** to name the process.","Choose **preparing**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"After preposition **of**, use **preparing** to name the process.","B":"A base verb does not supply the noun-like complement after of.","C":"A to-infinitive cannot directly follow of in this pattern.","D":"A past form does not name the process after of."}}},{"id":"6aaca44d-ddc0-58ff-af1c-3cb959651eea","topicSlug":"linking-clauses","level":"B1","prompt":"“We were tired ____ we had repaired the rowing boat all morning.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"despite"},{"id":"B","text":"during"},{"id":"C","text":"because"},{"id":"D","text":"because of"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"The reason has its own **subject and verb**.","explanation":"**Because** introduces the reason as a finite clause: we had ... .","keywords":["because-finite-clause"],"skill":"because-finite-clause","contextKey":"linking-clauses-50","fingerprint":"453d0a118b0e8c5e183433583c90fd9dbd5cd37d563ab7e2bed2be24cd65a46d","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["dfe-grammar"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The reason has its own **subject and verb**.","**Because** introduces the reason as a finite clause: we had ... .","Choose **because**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Despite would need a noun phrase and would express contrast, not the stated reason.","B":"During takes a noun phrase, not a finite clause.","C":"**Because** introduces the reason as a finite clause: we had ... .","D":"Because of normally takes a noun phrase or -ing expression, not this finite clause."}}},{"id":"dbc10698-d6c6-5991-a0a9-95afcd0223af","topicSlug":"linking-clauses","level":"B1","prompt":"“We could not repair the rowing boat ____ the power cut.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"although"},{"id":"B","text":"while"},{"id":"C","text":"because of"},{"id":"D","text":"because"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"The reason is a **noun phrase**, so choose because of.","explanation":"The reason is a noun phrase, **the power cut**, so use **because of**.","keywords":["because-of-noun"],"skill":"because-of-noun","contextKey":"linking-clauses-50","fingerprint":"58496cea899a2622443cff3703cc89c5ec97e97f521f1dd3a956608601aadadb","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["dfe-grammar"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The reason is a **noun phrase**, so choose because of.","The reason is a noun phrase, **the power cut**, so use **because of**.","Choose **because of**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Although normally introduces a finite clause and gives contrast rather than this reason.","B":"While would normally introduce a clause, not this bare reason phrase.","C":"The reason is a noun phrase, **the power cut**, so use **because of**.","D":"Because requires a finite reason clause rather than this noun phrase alone."}}},{"id":"61ca93c2-a6bb-5c59-a06e-731d189128ac","topicSlug":"linking-clauses","level":"B1","prompt":"Express purpose with a different subject: “I brought the instructions ____ you could repair the rowing boat.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"in order to"},{"id":"B","text":"because of"},{"id":"C","text":"despite"},{"id":"D","text":"so that"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"The purpose clause has its own subject **you** and modal **could**.","explanation":"**So that** introduces a finite purpose clause whose subject is you.","keywords":["purpose-so-that"],"skill":"purpose-so-that","contextKey":"linking-clauses-50","fingerprint":"05f66448173b9c1581c90a737c47c6559ef995825f3c7fa3be829646dbb29a15","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["dfe-grammar"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The purpose clause has its own subject **you** and modal **could**.","**So that** introduces a finite purpose clause whose subject is you.","Choose **so that**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"In order to needs a base verb immediately, not you could.","B":"Because of cannot introduce this finite purpose clause.","C":"Despite both changes the relation and requires a different complement.","D":"**So that** introduces a finite purpose clause whose subject is you."}}},{"id":"d2c48370-e29b-58dd-a153-d5a203238b74","topicSlug":"linking-clauses","level":"B1","prompt":"Use the time preposition before an -ing noun phrase: “The error occurred during the process of ____ the rowing boat.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"repaired"},{"id":"B","text":"repairing"},{"id":"C","text":"repair"},{"id":"D","text":"to repair"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"The preposition **of** needs a noun-like complement: use the **-ing form**.","explanation":"After preposition **of**, use **repairing** to name the process.","keywords":["during-while"],"skill":"during-while","contextKey":"linking-clauses-50","fingerprint":"bcb08a747cc539f3818de997265f613020bf0991145240fcae3e084b525fa5d9","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["dfe-grammar"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The preposition **of** needs a noun-like complement: use the **-ing form**.","After preposition **of**, use **repairing** to name the process.","Choose **repairing**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"A past form does not name the process after of.","B":"After preposition **of**, use **repairing** to name the process.","C":"A base verb does not supply the noun-like complement after of.","D":"A to-infinitive cannot directly follow of in this pattern."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Connect ideas with the right kind of clause or phrase, and keep their logical relationship clear.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Coordinating complete ideas","explanation":"And, but and or can connect words, phrases or clauses of equal status. When linking two independent clauses, punctuation helps mark the boundary. Choose the connector from the intended relation: addition, contrast or alternative.","pattern":"We checked the route, **and** we booked the tickets.","commonMistakes":["A connector should express the intended relation; grammatical sentences can still communicate the wrong logic."],"examples":["“We were tired **because** we had prepared the visitor guide all morning.”","“We were tired **because** we had restored the old portrait all morning.”"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Reasons: because and because of","explanation":"Because introduces a finite clause with a subject and verb. Because of introduces a noun phrase or an -ing expression. The meaning may be similar, but the grammar following the connector differs.","pattern":"**Because it rained**, we stayed. / **Because of the rain**, we stayed.","commonMistakes":["Because of it rained puts a finite clause after a preposition phrase without the structure needed for it."],"examples":["“We could not prepare the visitor guide **because of** the power cut.”","“We could not restore the old portrait **because of** the power cut.”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Purpose and result","explanation":"A to-infinitive often gives purpose when its understood subject matches the main subject. So that can introduce a finite purpose clause, especially when the subjects differ. So and therefore can introduce results, but therefore behaves as an adverb and needs suitable sentence punctuation.","pattern":"I spoke slowly **so that everyone could follow**.","commonMistakes":["Therefore cannot always replace so between two clauses with the same punctuation; avoid a comma splice."],"examples":["Express purpose with a different subject: “I brought the instructions **so that** you could prepare the visitor guide.”","Express purpose with a different subject: “I brought the instructions **so that** you could restore the old portrait.”"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Time clauses and sequence","explanation":"When, while, before, after, until and as soon as describe different temporal relations. While often accompanies an activity in progress; until describes continuation up to a point. In an ordinary future time clause, use a present form rather than will.","pattern":"I’ll wait **until you arrive**.","commonMistakes":["By Friday gives a deadline; until Friday says something continues to Friday. They are not interchangeable."],"examples":["Use the time preposition before an -ing noun phrase: “The error occurred during the process of **preparing** the visitor guide.”","Use the time preposition before an -ing noun phrase: “The error occurred during the process of **restoring** the old portrait.”"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["We checked the route, **and** we booked the tickets.","**Because it rained**, we stayed. / **Because of the rain**, we stayed.","I spoke slowly **so that everyone could follow**.","I’ll wait **until you arrive**."]},"stage":"4 · Connecting meanings","prerequisites":["wishes"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
